('h1', 'Solution Overview'),
('p', 'This chapter presents SERMS through complementary views: the system context and containers (C4 levels 1-2), tiers and layers, components (C4 level 3), the logical and physical deployment, the domain model (DDD), the data model and the detailed design of two critical use cases.'),
('fig', '01-system-context', 'System context (C4 level 1): five human roles, the scheduler and external systems'),
('h2', 'Logical Architecture & Design'),
('p', 'Logically, SERMS is one deployable application organised into ten business modules that mirror the bounded contexts of Section 3.1.4. The browser runs a React single-page application; nginx serves it and load-balances `/api/*` calls across identical, stateless Spring Boot replicas; PostgreSQL 18 is the single source of truth. Figure 2 shows the containers and their protocols.'),
('fig', '02-containers', 'Container view (C4 level 2): scalable modular monolith behind a load balancer', {'max_h': 8.0}),
('h3', 'Key Architectural Decisions'),
('p', 'The key architectural decisions taken are as follows:'),
('tab', ['Identifier', 'Description'], [
    ['AD-01', '**Modular monolith instead of microservices.** One Spring Boot application with module boundaries per bounded context. Trade-off: one deployable and ACID transactions across reservation, loan and maintenance (needed for overlap and lock-order guarantees) in exchange for coarser scaling and a shared release cadence. Microservice extraction is prepared through ports and domain events (Section 6).'],
    ['AD-02', '**Stateless application tier for horizontal scaling.** No user state is kept in JVM memory: HTTP sessions are stored in PostgreSQL with Spring Session JDBC, and scheduled jobs take a ShedLock row lock. Any replica can serve any request, so replicas can be added, removed or restarted behind the load balancer without sticky sessions.'],
    ['AD-03', '**Separate SPA and REST API on the same origin.** React talks to `/api/v1` through nginx on the same host name. This avoids CORS and lets the browser use an HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite session cookie with a CSRF header instead of storing JWTs in JavaScript.'],
    ['AD-04', '**Four-layer modules (api, service, domain, repository).** Controllers never touch repositories; business rules live in domain classes; services own transactions and `@PreAuthorize` checks. Modules call each other only through public services or ports.'],
    ['AD-05', '**Domain events for cross-module side effects.** Reservation, approval, loan and maintenance publish events; notification and audit subscribe. Audit and notification requests are written in the same transaction, and delivery runs asynchronously after commit.'],
    ['AD-06', '**OpenAPI contract as the single API definition.** The backend generates OpenAPI 3; the front end generates TypeScript types from it and CI fails on drift, so field mismatches fail compilation instead of production.'],
], W2, 'Logical architectural decisions'),
('h3', 'Tiers and Layers'),
('p', 'SERMS uses four physical tiers and, inside the application tier, four logical layers. Figure 3 shows the responsibilities of each and the protocols between them.'),
