('fig', '09-domain-events', 'Domain event flow: producers, events and consumers'),
('p', '**Producers and consumers.** Inside the monolith events travel through Spring\'s `ApplicationEventPublisher`. Listeners that must be consistent with the business change (audit, notification request) run in the producer\'s transaction; delivery to the user runs later in `NotificationWorker`, with bounded retries and a de-duplication key enforced by a unique constraint. Because every event already has a stable identifier and payload, the same events can be written to an outbox table and published to a broker when contexts are extracted as services (Section 6).'),

('h2', 'Physical Architecture & Design'),
('p', 'SERMS runs on one Linux virtual machine in Hong Kong with Docker Engine and Docker Compose. Host nginx terminates TLS and forwards to the `web` container on the loopback interface. The `web` container serves the SPA and load-balances API calls over N `app` replicas on an internal Docker network; the database container has no published port. Monitoring and backups run on a separate ops network. Figure 9 shows the physical view.'),
('fig', '06-deployment-physical', 'Physical deployment view (production)', {'max_h': 8.3}),
('h3', 'Key Architectural Decisions'),
('p', 'The key architectural decisions taken are as follows:'),
('tab', ['Identifier', 'Description'], [
    ['AD-07', '**nginx as the load balancer, with Docker DNS discovery.** `upstream app_pool { zone; least_conn; server app:8081 resolve max_fails=0; keepalive 32; }`. The resolver re-reads Docker DNS every 2 s, so replicas join and leave without an nginx reload. A failed connect moves to the next replica (`proxy_next_upstream`, 1 s connect timeout, 10 s budget). A GET/HEAD that nginx itself could not deliver is replayed once. Measured: 0 failed requests during rolling updates and after a replica was killed. Trade-off: no extra component, and the same nginx already serves the SPA. The cost is fewer features than HAProxy or a cloud load balancer, with no active health checks in open-source nginx.'],
    ['AD-08', '**Sessions in PostgreSQL (Spring Session JDBC) rather than sticky sessions or Redis.** Sticky sessions break on failover; Redis adds a stateful component to operate. Sessions are small and few (campus scale), so one indexed table is enough; Spring Session deletes expired rows every minute. The cookie keeps the contract name `JSESSIONID` and its HttpOnly, Secure and SameSite=Lax attributes. Revisit when session reads exceed a few hundred per second.'],
    ['AD-09', '**Docker Compose on one VM instead of Kubernetes.** The Proposal excluded Kubernetes; Compose `deploy.replicas: ${SERMS_APP_REPLICAS:-2}` and `scripts/rolling-update.sh` give horizontal scaling and rolling replacement at far lower operating cost. The VM is a single point of failure, accepted for a campus pilot (Section 3.4).'],
    ['AD-10', '**Network segmentation.** Three Docker networks: `frontend` (web, publishes 127.0.0.1:8789 only), `backend` with `internal: true` (web, app, db, Prometheus), and `ops` (Prometheus, Grafana on loopback ports). Only host nginx is reachable from the Internet. The app port and `/api/v1/prometheus` are never exposed; nginx answers 404 for the metrics path.'],
    ['AD-11', '**Immutable, SHA-tagged images built from one multi-stage Dockerfile.** `serms-app:<sha>` and `serms-web:<sha>` run as non-root users; the same SHA is scanned by Trivy in CI and shown in the UI footer, so rollback means starting the previous tag.'],
], W2, 'Physical architectural decisions'),
